nina: quick sync 10am POC with Orion Retail. They want a minimal integration: python SSE stream -> mid-stream tool call to inventory search -> structured JSON back to client and continue text stream. Also need a short note on how to handle partial JSON across chunks.

raj: ok. Which SDK are they using?

nina: they9re on python 3.10 and will use the redwood-sdk. want something copy-paste for eng.

kevin: two patterns: 1) coarse-grained: buffer until tool_call_end and then emit a single tool_result event; 2) incremental: send incremental tool_result_chunk events with sequence numbers and a final tool_result_end. incremental works better for large results.

raj: example coarse pattern (SSE semantics):
```py
# pseudo-server-side handler
from redwood import Client
c = Client(api_key="${POC_KEY}")
with c.stream(model="rwd-medium", mode="sse") as s:
    for ev in s:
        if ev.type == "tool_call_start":
            call_id = ev.call_id
            parts = []
        elif ev.type == "tool_call_chunk":
            parts.append(ev.text)
        elif ev.type == "tool_call_end":
            payload = "".join(parts)
            result = inventory.search(payload)
            # emit structured event that clients can JSON.parse
            s.emit_event('tool_result', {"call_id": call_id, "result": result})
        else:
            client_forward(ev)
```

kevin: for partial JSON across chunks: ensure each chunk is clearly labeled (chunk_index / total or is_final boolean). If you must stream raw JSON fragments, wrap them in a higher-level envelope event so clients can reassemble safely.

nina: can you give a tiny incremental example that the client (browser) can consume without complex buffering?

raj: yes. server sends events with event: tool_result_chunk and data: {call_id, seq, text, is_final:false} and then final event with is_final:true. client just appends seq in order and when is_final true it JSON.parse the joined text. If you want to avoid parse errors from partials, base64-encode chunks.

kevin: sample client-side logic (browser):
```js
const es = new EventSource('/stream?key=${POC_KEY}');
const pending = {};
es.onmessage = (e) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(e.data);
  if (msg.type === 'tool_result_chunk') {
    const c = pending[msg.call_id] || [];
    c[msg.seq] = msg.text;
    pending[msg.call_id] = c;
    if (msg.is_final) {
      const joined = c.join('');
      const decoded = msg.is_base64 ? atob(joined) : joined;
      const parsed = JSON.parse(decoded);
      handleToolResult(parsed);
      delete pending[msg.call_id];
    }
  } else {
    renderToken(msg.text);
  }
};
```

infra-bot: note: sample-client 1.12+ includes server helpers for `emit_event('tool_result')` and chunking utilities.

nina: security/GRC asked about temporary keys and logs for the POC. What do we give them?

kevin: give them STS-style short-lived tokens (30m-1h) + IP allowlist for the POC. Make sure the stream endpoint logs request ids and call_id metadata so we can trace any tool_result attachments. For storing tool outputs, use customer KMS on dedicated/private; for hosted POC just mark ephemeral outputs as non-persistent and redact sensitive fields.

raj: also call out perf notes: if you need low latency while debugging, set batch_size=1 and disable large auto-batching on the server side. For production, use batching and prefix caching to save tokens.

nina: awesome. Can one of you paste the demo repo link (tiny python server + browser client) into this thread and a one-paragraph security blurb?

kevin: pushing a minimal gist now. gist.github.com/redwood/poc-orion-stream-demo -> includes server, client, and run instructions.

kevin: security blurb: "For POC deployments we issue short-lived API keys (recommended TTL 30-60 minutes) and enable TLS + IP allowlist. All stream events include a request_id and optional call_id to correlate tool usage. Do not persist tool outputs in hosted POC; if persistence is required use enterprise plan with customer-managed KMS. Audit logs for all tool_result events are retained for 30 days and can be exported on request."

nina: perfect. putting this in the SOW appendix. thanks both. :sparkles:
